00:33:36,040 --> 00:33:38,100
Ok, the microphone there.

161
00:33:39,600 --> 00:33:53,460
I shared what you said and I was worried because before Madam Scotland mentioned, I mean, nobody seemed to be worried by the results of the last election in May.

162
00:33:54,240 --> 00:34:07,460
And if you don't find some kind of solution, short-term solution in five years, the number of voters that would be against Europe would be the majority.

163
00:34:07,460 --> 00:34:19,320
So this is the real need, I think, together with the greater breadth and the larger initiatives, there must be a change of course.

164
00:34:19,600 --> 00:34:28,120
We are in a storm, we have to change course and make sure that the people next time will vote for Europe and not against.

165
00:34:32,080 --> 00:34:38,800
I think that he has something to say about the role of the Court of Justice.

166
00:34:42,200 --> 00:34:43,580
Why don't you react?

167
00:34:45,000 --> 00:34:54,060
Well, no, I have to say that I found quite interesting the remark on the constitutionalisation of the treaty made by the Court of Justice.

168
00:34:54,060 --> 00:34:56,200
It is in a way true.

169
00:34:56,860 --> 00:35:13,240
The question is, this constitutionalisation of the treaty decided by judges, was it compatible with the existing, with the frame existing at the time?

170
00:35:14,480 --> 00:35:20,720
Has it become compatible because it has been accepted for decades?

171
00:35:21,520 --> 00:35:39,180
Could it be put now into question, considering that if we put that into question, we enter into a situation where one of the grounds of what exists now at the European level,

172
00:35:39,760 --> 00:35:45,040
one of the glue of Europe as it is now, is really put into question?

173
00:35:45,700 --> 00:35:52,880
I wonder about the consequences, even the practical consequences of your statement.

174
00:35:58,420 --> 00:36:02,160
Romano, can we have the honour of a comment from you?

175
00:36:04,260 --> 00:36:04,780
No?

176
00:36:09,400 --> 00:36:11,560
Your no is not credible.

177
00:36:11,560 --> 00:36:15,120
I am not, as you know, an illegal expert.

178
00:36:16,920 --> 00:36:28,040
What I am afraid is I take the last sentence of Antonio that if we go on like we are going now,

179
00:36:28,560 --> 00:36:35,820
we have no time for this long term change, academic discussion,

180
00:36:36,150 --> 00:36:46,380
because the last sentence of Antonio is the sentence now, as we shall examine tomorrow morning.

181
00:36:48,560 --> 00:37:05,800
What do you think about the decision taken by the European Central Bank during the crisis?

182
00:37:07,280 --> 00:37:15,780
As far as I understand, the only innovation, I don't want to be more precise about innovation,

183
00:37:16,380 --> 00:37:20,500
in these years has been done by the European Central Bank.

184
00:37:21,420 --> 00:37:30,020
Are these innovations compatible with the statute, with the treaties,

185
00:37:30,840 --> 00:37:37,320
and in some cases with the fundamental law of some country or not?

186
00:37:38,080 --> 00:37:43,200
Looking to the innovation from the economic point of view, these are not innovations,

187
00:37:43,440 --> 00:37:49,690
these are just obvious things to do and not enough, just obvious things to do.

188
00:37:49,690 --> 00:37:55,330
But the question is there and the question means that there is also danger.

189
00:37:56,810 --> 00:38:03,610
Then somehow someone will say, well, you have done something which is against the treaties,

190
00:38:04,090 --> 00:38:09,210
against the statute and against some fundamental law or some country of the euro area.

191
00:38:11,270 --> 00:38:12,630
Whatever it takes.

192
00:38:14,250 --> 00:38:15,210
C'è una domanda?

193
00:38:17,390 --> 00:38:21,430
No, si stava grattando il mento, sorry.

194
00:38:24,730 --> 00:38:26,370
Is there anybody else?

195
00:38:28,030 --> 00:38:36,310
Ok, if there are no other questions, I give each of you five minutes in the reverse order as usual.

196
00:38:37,050 --> 00:38:41,350
Antonio, you are the first one and then Renaud will close.

197
00:38:41,350 --> 00:38:45,530
Well, thank you so much, I will be very brief.

198
00:38:46,330 --> 00:38:53,870
On the constitutional and economic question, I believe that the amount of political capital

199
00:38:53,870 --> 00:39:00,630
that would be required to make a major amendment of the treaties

200
00:39:01,150 --> 00:39:08,330
should be better used in focusing on an economic agenda in the short run

201
00:39:08,330 --> 00:39:12,890
that has some institutional and constitutional implications.

202
00:39:13,910 --> 00:39:18,450
But the departing point is not a discussion among constitutional lawyers,

203
00:39:19,030 --> 00:39:25,990
the departing point should be about, yes, we want to build a single monetary zone

204
00:39:25,990 --> 00:39:32,430
that can work even if it does not fit in the criteria that the economists have defined

205
00:39:32,430 --> 00:39:35,270
as a so-called optimal monetary zone.

206
00:39:35,270 --> 00:39:42,510
I will not have time to elaborate on the details, but this is precisely the key issue.

207
00:39:42,530 --> 00:39:46,130
We have to demonstrate that we can have a single currency

208
00:39:46,970 --> 00:39:52,930
that corrects the imbalances that exist in the area,

209
00:39:53,270 --> 00:39:56,270
economic, competitive imbalances,

210
00:39:56,910 --> 00:40:01,090
and that we have the necessary political will to overcome those difficulties.

211
00:40:01,270 --> 00:40:03,730
This will raise the issue of the euro area budget

212
00:40:04,530 --> 00:40:11,350
and the question of what kind of growth-enhanced reforms are we really in need

213
00:40:11,350 --> 00:40:16,190
to improve competitiveness, economic growth and create jobs.

214
00:40:16,650 --> 00:40:17,790
That's the key agenda

215
00:40:17,790 --> 00:40:22,510
and there will be some institutional consequences of that that I will not elaborate now.

216
00:40:22,810 --> 00:40:25,870
Second point, on the ECB, to be honest,

217
00:40:27,170 --> 00:40:32,150
I think that a lot depends on what the European Court of Justice is going to decide

218
00:40:32,150 --> 00:40:35,390
on the outright monetary transaction decision.

219
00:40:37,110 --> 00:40:41,930
The good news is that for the first time the German Constitutional Court accepted

220
00:40:41,930 --> 00:40:46,360
to raise a preliminary ruling in Luxembourg

221
00:40:46,510 --> 00:40:49,290
and that's quite a revolution.

222
00:40:51,050 --> 00:40:57,830
Secondly, the Karlsruhe Court has already explained to the European Court of Justice

223
00:40:57,830 --> 00:41:01,730
what's going to be the potential interpretation of Karlsruhe

224
00:41:01,730 --> 00:41:05,110
on what might be the decision of the European Court of Justice.

225
00:41:05,550 --> 00:41:07,770
So the European Court of Justice cannot hide

226
00:41:08,400 --> 00:41:11,890
because they know what they are expected to decide.

227
00:41:13,310 --> 00:41:21,790
I believe that the ECB has put the test of the treaties to the limits.

228
00:41:23,410 --> 00:41:27,590
Yes, there are grey zones there, yes there are,

229
00:41:28,010 --> 00:41:29,690
but also the European Council.

230
00:41:30,290 --> 00:41:32,910
Let's be very honest, if I was a lawyer

231
00:41:34,830 --> 00:41:37,890
I would say to Prime Minister David Cameron

232
00:41:37,890 --> 00:41:41,630
you should go to the European Court of Justice

233
00:41:41,630 --> 00:41:45,590
when those guys have decided that the European institutions

234
00:41:45,590 --> 00:41:48,390
could be allocated to the Eurozone alone.

235
00:41:48,390 --> 00:41:52,420
That was David Cameron's argument in December 2012

236
00:41:53,530 --> 00:41:55,090
against the fiscal compact.

237
00:41:56,190 --> 00:42:01,480
And as a lawyer I could present to him some arguments

238
00:42:01,850 --> 00:42:03,590
in defense of its view.

239
00:42:04,350 --> 00:42:06,330
He did not go to the court, by the way.

240
00:42:07,610 --> 00:42:09,010
And that's quite an innovation.

241
00:42:09,630 --> 00:42:13,190
I mean the British, even though they are very Euroceptic,

242
00:42:13,310 --> 00:42:17,730
they have accepted the principle of the fiscal compact.

243
00:42:17,730 --> 00:42:22,070
And that's a major innovation, in my view,

244
00:42:24,190 --> 00:42:26,630
that has created a precedent

245
00:42:26,630 --> 00:42:31,470
that can be used in the future in other policy areas.

246
00:42:40,710 --> 00:42:45,130
Just to give a clear-cut answer to Alberto

247
00:42:47,050 --> 00:42:49,410
on the central bank

248
00:42:50,070 --> 00:42:53,250
I'm convinced that one of the main arguments

249
00:42:53,250 --> 00:42:57,110
used by the court of Karl Schruhe

250
00:42:57,690 --> 00:42:59,970
was to argue that

251
00:43:01,110 --> 00:43:03,950
what the bank had been doing

252
00:43:03,950 --> 00:43:06,250
was not monetary policy

253
00:43:06,430 --> 00:43:09,250
which is within its prerogatives

254
00:43:09,250 --> 00:43:11,490
but financial policy

255
00:43:11,490 --> 00:43:15,230
which is in the Member States Government's prerogative

256
00:43:15,230 --> 00:43:16,570
is not correct.

257
00:43:17,370 --> 00:43:19,990
And I have said it quite informally

258
00:43:19,990 --> 00:43:23,910
to my colleagues of the Bundesverfassungsgericht

259
00:43:24,520 --> 00:43:29,710
the argument runs like this

260
00:43:29,710 --> 00:43:34,150
buying state bonds is allowed

261
00:43:34,150 --> 00:43:36,190
is part of monetary policy

262
00:43:36,190 --> 00:43:42,430
as long as there is no discrimination among the bonds that are bought

263
00:43:42,430 --> 00:43:49,630
while if you are selecting certain states and not others

264
00:43:49,630 --> 00:43:51,330
this is financial policy.

265
00:43:51,330 --> 00:43:52,570
This is not correct

266
00:43:53,010 --> 00:43:57,290
because monetary policy has the main mission

267
00:43:57,290 --> 00:44:01,570
to promote interest rates

268
00:44:01,570 --> 00:44:06,390
compatible with the target of the central bank.

269
00:44:06,930 --> 00:44:11,510
Now, if there are interest rates that are much higher

270
00:44:11,510 --> 00:44:17,810
these are the interest rates that I should remove or change

271
00:44:18,110 --> 00:44:20,590
and these are the interest rates

272
00:44:20,590 --> 00:44:25,590
that the market practices versus the bonds of some states

273
00:44:25,590 --> 00:44:27,390
and not of all the states

274
00:44:27,390 --> 00:44:31,130
and this is monetary policy, not financial policy

275
00:44:31,130 --> 00:44:33,630
this is my very simple view.

276
00:44:34,450 --> 00:44:35,530
Second point

277
00:44:37,170 --> 00:44:40,700
I had a note here, I haven't mentioned it

278
00:44:41,410 --> 00:44:45,540
in which I was somehow stating

279
00:44:46,280 --> 00:44:55,500
that what Joe Weiler had written more than 10 years ago

280
00:44:55,500 --> 00:45:02,480
when he wrote that the Grundnorm of the European Constitution

281
00:45:02,480 --> 00:45:05,340
even not a written document

282
00:45:05,340 --> 00:45:08,400
was constitutional tolerance

283
00:45:08,860 --> 00:45:12,460
my point is he was right.

284
00:45:12,850 --> 00:45:16,640
You might think that constitutional tolerance

285
00:45:16,640 --> 00:45:20,490
which means accepting our diversities

286
00:45:21,300 --> 00:45:25,320
and staying together independently of

287
00:45:25,320 --> 00:45:30,470
is not the same thing as the principle

288
00:45:30,840 --> 00:45:35,900
that Patricia has stated a few minutes ago

289
00:45:35,900 --> 00:45:38,500
let us stay together

290
00:45:38,500 --> 00:45:45,740
let us use the 99% we have in common as a platform

291
00:45:46,060 --> 00:45:51,540
now this is exactly the constitutional tolerance

292
00:45:51,860 --> 00:45:56,600
because we don't accept our diversities

293
00:45:56,780 --> 00:46:01,080
when we don't recognize what we have in common

294
00:46:01,080 --> 00:46:05,780
at that point we become intolerant

295
00:46:06,140 --> 00:46:10,620
and our main principle

296
00:46:10,620 --> 00:46:12,920
which is freedom of movement

297
00:46:13,100 --> 00:46:16,080
is at that point at risk

298
00:46:16,580 --> 00:46:20,040
and this is precisely what is happening now

299
00:46:21,060 --> 00:46:24,140
perhaps not peace among us

